From: Circle OS Build <ops@circleos.org>
Date: 2026-05-30
Subject: [PATCH 0001/000X] systemserver: start CirclePrivacyService

Wires CirclePrivacyService (vendor/circle/privacy/) into the SystemServer
boot sequence so the "circle_privacy" binder is published before any
third-party app starts.

We start it via the String overload of SystemServiceManager.startService
so SystemServer does not need an import of a Circle-owned class -- the
service is reflectively loaded from services.circleprivacy at boot.

If the start throws, we reportWtf and continue: a broken Circle service
must never prevent SystemServer from finishing its boot.

Targets: AOSP 15, tag android-15.0.0_r20.
Touches: services/java/com/android/server/SystemServer.java

diff --git a/services/java/com/android/server/SystemServer.java b/services/java/com/android/server/SystemServer.java
index 92dbf63b3cf7..139261cc882b 100644
--- a/services/java/com/android/server/SystemServer.java
+++ b/services/java/com/android/server/SystemServer.java
@@ -1484,6 +1484,19 @@ public final class SystemServer implements Dumpable {
         t.traceBegin("startOtherServices");
         mSystemServiceManager.updateOtherServicesStartIndex();
 
+        // ---- Circle OS services -----------------------------------------
+        // Start the Privacy Framework as early as possible so its binder
+        // is available before any third-party app boots. See
+        // vendor/circle/privacy/.
+        try {
+            t.traceBegin("StartCirclePrivacyService");
+            mSystemServiceManager.startService(
+                    "com.circleos.privacy.CirclePrivacyService");
+            t.traceEnd();
+        } catch (Throwable e) {
+            reportWtf("starting CirclePrivacyService", e);
+        }
+
         final Context context = mSystemContext;
         DynamicSystemService dynamicSystem = null;
         IStorageManager storageManager = null;
